hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today's lesson and on the one  gene one enzyme hypothesis this is a  hypothesis that was first proposed in  1941 by two scientists whose names were  Beetle and Tatum and they had this idea  that each gene in the genome coded for  an individual enzyme and specifically  that each one of these genes that coded  for each individual enzyme and each  individual enzymes us impacted a  specific step in a metabolic pathway  what do we mean by a metabolic pathway  well for example the synthesis of the  amino acid arginine in south is a  stepwise process in so the precursor and  then this precursors is transformed into  other things one after the other until  arguing is eventually synthesized by the  cell and so we went and had this idea  that there were these gene decoded for  an enzyme and that enzyme would catalyze  one step in this process and that  another gene would code for another  enzyme that catalyzes another staff and  another gene was code for another  environment catalyzed another step and  so on and so forth and they were correct  in that these kinds of gene and  metabolic pathways certainly to exist  and are very important to cellular  function and their idea that one can  contribute to metabolic pathways in this  manner through this one gene one enzyme  interface with a major contribution to  the field of early molecular genetics  however we now understand it to be kind  of an oversimplification and we know the  real processes in a Cell are much more  complicated so for example we now know  that many genes encode nan and dine  proteins so for example proteins like  insulin which is a signaling hormone it  helps to regulate blood sugar or protein  like keratin which is a structural  protein it gives strength to hair and  skin and nails or transport proteins  like for example hemoglobin which is a  protein in red blood cells that helps to  transport oxygen and carbon dioxide  between the lungs and the tissues and  also proteins and antibodies which are  important in immunity and helping us to  fight off pathogens so these are all  examples of proteins which are coded for  by genes but which are not actually  enzyme which is which is a certain major  exceptions this one gene one enzyme  hypothesis another major exception is  that many proteins are actually composed  of more than one polypeptide and the  Echenique polypeptides is coded by a  separate gene so actually all of the  proteins that I mentioned up here from  insulin to keratin to hemoglobin to  antibodies these are all composed with  at least two polypeptides if not three  or four and each of these is coded by a  separate hmm and so this gives us a  different way of looking at this  original hypothesis knowing that each  gene can code for different polypeptides  so that the final proteins will contain  components coded for by many genes it's  also true there are many genes that code  for various types of non-coding RNAs  what do I mean when I say non-coding  well we typically think that a gene  codes for protein in that the gene is  transcribed in the process of  transcription to make an mRNA transcript  and that mRNA transcript is then  translated protein it may not apply to  them during the process of translation  so that is that pathway for gene  expression in the stuff but there are  certainly many genes that code for  various types of non-coding RNA so for  example genes that code for our RNA  the catalytic portion of the ribosome  genes that code for tRNAs which are the  transfer RNAs is bringing amino acids to  the ribosome during that process of  translation that I just mentioned  there's also a whole host of regulatory  RNA many many different kinds micro RNA  small interfering RNAs long non-coding  RNAs and more than I can possibly  mention here and they have a wide  variety of regulatory functions in the  cell they're very very critical to  cellular function if you're interested  in learning more about these different  types of regulatory RNA please see my  video on that topic  another exception to the original one  gene one enzyme hypothesis by Beadle and  Tatum is that many multi exonic genes  produce transcripts that are  alternatively spliced what does this  mean well multi exonic means a gene that  would transcribe into an mRNA there are  more than one exon there and these  different exponents can be joins  together or spliced out in differential  patterns or process of alternative  splicing which means that from one gene  that creates one mRNA transcript many  different proteins can result if you're  interested in learning more about  alternative splicing and how that  increases protein diversity in a cell  then please see my video on alternative  splicing that is it for today and our  discussion of the one gene one enzyme  hypothesis thanks for watching biology  professors
 

